sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant p1 as p1 source producer
    participant c1 as c1-0 topic-partition
    participant p2 as p2 consumes c1, sinks c4
    participant c4 as c4-0 topic-partition
    participant p3 as p3 consumes c4, sinks c2
    participant c2 as c2-0 topic-partition
    participant p4 as p4 this node, consumes c1 and c2 only

    Note over p1,p4: 1. The chain m1 to m2 to m3 is built, running through c4 which p4 never consumes.

    p1->>c1: append m1 at c1-0 @7, stamp {}
    Note right of p1: A producer that stamps nothing claims nothing,<br/>so m1 is causally minimal by definition (E3).

    c1-->>p2: poll returns m1 at c1-0 @7
    p2->>p2: gate: no dependencies, deliver m1
    p2->>p2: merge m1, frontier {c1-0: 7}
    p2->>c4: append m2 at c4-0 @4, stamp {c1-0: 7}
    Note right of p2: I2, stamp transitive completeness. The stamp<br/>dominates the dependency clocks AND the coordinates<br/>of every delivered event, so m1's own position<br/>c1-0 @7 rides outward on m2.

    Note over p1,p4: 2. p3 consumes no c1. The gate ignores that coordinate, the merge keeps it.

    c4-->>p3: poll returns m2 at c4-0 @4, deps {c1-0: 7}
    p3->>p3: gate c1-0: not consumed, ignore, count the metric
    p3->>p3: gate: no consumed dependency left, deliver m2
    p3->>p3: merge m2 unconditionally, state {c1-0: 7, c4-0: 4}
    Note right of p3: I9, unconditional merge. p3 retains c1-0 @7<br/>although it has no c1 subscription and its own gate<br/>ignored that entry. The gate may ignore, the merge<br/>may not. This is the cross-node inductive step.
    p3->>c2: append m3 at c2-0 @9, stamp {c1-0: 7, c4-0: 4}
    Note right of p3: m3 now claims m1 DIRECTLY, by its own coordinate,<br/>not merely by proxy through c4-0. That direct claim<br/>is the entry p4 will enforce.

    Note over p1,p4: 3. p4 evaluates m3. The gate is total: one branch ignores, the other holds.

    c2-->>p4: poll returns m3 at c2-0 @9, deps {c1-0: 7, c4-0: 4}
    p4->>p4: gate c4-0: not consumed, ignore, count the metric
    p4->>p4: gate c1-0: consumed, require frontier >= 7
    Note right of p4: p4's contiguous delivered frontier stands at c1-0 @6.<br/>The direct claim is unsatisfied, so m3 is held. Only<br/>p4's own delivery can satisfy it, never a claim carried<br/>on a sibling channel. Local delivery, never hearsay.
    p4->>p4: hold m3 in the buffer, no timeout, no eviction

    Note over p1,p4: 4. The cause arrives on p4's own subscription and releases the effect.

    c1-->>p4: poll returns m1 at c1-0 @7
    p4->>p4: gate: no dependencies, deliver m1
    p4->>p4: merge m1, frontier advances to {c1-0: 7}
    p4->>p4: re-evaluate held records, 7 >= 7 satisfied
    p4->>p4: deliver m3, after m1, causal order preserved
    Note right of p4: Ignoring c4-0 lost no ordering observable at p4.<br/>The ordering that ran through c4 was already stated<br/>explicitly by c1-0 @7, which I9 guaranteed would<br/>be present. Sound, not vacuously satisfied.

    Note over p1,p4: THE COUNTERFACTUAL<br/>Suppose p3's merge were not unconditional, and it dropped c1-0 @7 as out of scope.<br/>Then m3 would carry {c4-0: 4} alone. p4 would ignore that single entry, find nothing<br/>left to check, and deliver m3 immediately, ahead of m1. An effect before its cause.<br/><br/>The ignore branch is sound only in the presence of unconditional merge. That is why I9<br/>permits carried ancestry to be skipped but never dropped, and why the only coordinates<br/>allowed to leave stamp-feeding state are provably destroyed topic UUIDs, which no<br/>receiver could ever deliver.
